# Copy to .env. Never committed.

# **A value containing a dollar sign must be single-quoted.** Compose treats
# `$NAME` inside a .env value as a variable reference and substitutes an empty
# string for it — silently, with only a "variable is not set" warning on `up`.
# A password like `abc$V3hdef` arrives at the container as `abcdef`, and the
# service rejects it for no visible reason. Write it as 'abc$V3hdef', or double
# every dollar: abc$$V3hdef.

# --- Database ---------------------------------------------------------------
# On the server, compose assembles DATABASE_URL from this. Do not set
# DATABASE_URL there yourself.
#   openssl rand -hex 24
DB_PASSWORD=

# Local development only, where there is no compose interpolation. On the
# server compose's environment: block overrides this, and .dockerignore keeps
# .env out of the image entirely.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://steward:CHANGEME@localhost:5434/steward

# --- First user -------------------------------------------------------------
# Read once by prisma/seed.ts, then dead. The seeded password is temporary:
# the app forces a change on first sign-in.
SEED_EMAIL=
SEED_PASSWORD=
SEED_DISPLAY_NAME=Vincent

# --- Cookies ----------------------------------------------------------------
# "true" while Steward is reached over plain HTTP on the LAN. Remove it once
# every route in is HTTPS.
ALLOW_HTTP=true

# --- Unraid -----------------------------------------------------------------
# The icon shown in Unraid's Docker tab. Unraid keys it to the container name
# and fetches this URL, so it must be reachable when Steward itself is not.
STEWARD_ICON_URL=https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Pallarran/Steward/main/public/steward-icon.png

# --- Unraid -----------------------------------------------------------------
# The WhiteTower card on /systems: array usage, disk temperatures, read/write
# errors, and whatever parity is doing.
#
# Where the read-only bind mount lands inside the container — see
# docker-compose.yml. Steward runs on the Unraid box, so it reads the same
# .ini files the webGUI does. No plugin, no API key, no Unraid Connect.
#
# Leave it unset anywhere that is not the server: the card then says it is not
# connected, which is true, rather than showing an empty array as a healthy one.
UNRAID_STATE_DIR=/host/emhttp

# --- The server itself ------------------------------------------------------
# The Server card on /systems: uptime, load and memory from the host's own
# /proc, read through the bind mount in docker-compose.yml. Leave unset
# anywhere that is not the server and the card says it is not connected.
HOST_PROC_DIR=/host/proc

# The hardware half of the same card — health, temperatures and fans — read
# from the BMC over Redfish. Optional: with any of the three unset, those lines
# say the BMC is not configured and uptime and memory carry on working.
#
# **Use a read-only Redfish account, not the admin login.** Anything that can
# read Redfish with admin rights can also power-cycle the machine, and Steward
# has no reason to be able to do that. Create one in the BMC under
# Settings -> User Management with the Operator or ReadOnly role.
#
# The BMC's certificate is self-signed; the adapter accepts that for this one
# host and nothing else — see src/lib/adapters/server.ts.
BMC_BASE_URL=https://<bmc-lan-ip>
BMC_USER=
BMC_PASSWORD=

# --- Uptime Kuma ------------------------------------------------------------
# Read through /metrics, which needs an API key: Uptime Kuma -> profile menu
# -> Settings -> API Keys -> Add API Key. Sent as HTTP Basic with an empty
# username.
KUMA_BASE_URL=http://<whitetower-lan-ip>:1001
KUMA_KEY=

# --- Todoist ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Personal API token: Todoist -> Settings -> Integrations -> Developer.
# Sent as `Authorization: Bearer`. Read and write: ticking closes the task.
TODOIST_TOKEN=

# --- Home Assistant ---------------------------------------------------------
# Long-lived access token: HA -> your name in the sidebar -> Security ->
# Long-lived access tokens -> Create token.
#
# Use an IP, never a `.local` mDNS name. Those resolve on a desktop and not
# inside a container, and Home Assistant is not necessarily on the same host as
# Steward.
HA_BASE_URL=http://<home-assistant-ip>:8123
HA_TOKEN=

# --- Horizon ----------------------------------------------------------------
# The finance panel reads GET /api/summary — aggregate figures only, no
# holdings and no transactions.
#
# The key is shared: generate it once with `openssl rand -hex 32`, put it in
# Horizon's own .env as STEWARD_API_KEY, and the same value here. With it unset
# on either side, the panel says it is not connected and Horizon serves nothing.
HORIZON_BASE_URL=http://<whitetower-lan-ip>:3001
HORIZON_API_KEY=

# --- Paperless-ngx ----------------------------------------------------------
# The document search on /documents. Optional: with either unset, that section
# says it is not connected and the rest of the page works normally.
#
# Token: Paperless -> your profile (top right) -> API Auth Token. Sent as
# `Authorization: Token`, not Bearer.
#
# Steward never copies the archive. It asks Paperless when you ask it, so
# Paperless stays the document store — PRD §5.
PAPERLESS_BASE_URL=http://<whitetower-lan-ip>:8000
PAPERLESS_TOKEN=

# --- Local model (Ollama) ---------------------------------------------------
# Optional. With either unset, Settings says "not connected", nothing calls it,
# and every page works exactly as before — there is deliberately no default
# URL, so an install that has never been pointed at Ollama does not present as
# one that is broken.
#
# Steward runs in a container, so `localhost` here means the container, not the
# machine. Point it at the host's LAN address. If Ollama runs on the Docker
# host itself, `http://host.docker.internal:11434` also works, but on Linux
# that name only resolves when compose declares
# `extra_hosts: ["host.docker.internal:host-gateway"]` — the LAN address needs
# no such arrangement and is the safer of the two.
#
# Ollama binds to 127.0.0.1 by default and will refuse a container even on the
# same machine. It has to be started with OLLAMA_HOST=0.0.0.0 to be reachable.
#
# The model must be pulled: `ollama pull <name>`. Settings names it and says so
# when it is missing.
OLLAMA_BASE_URL=http://<ollama-host-ip>:11434
OLLAMA_MODEL=gemma3:12b

# --- Gmail ------------------------------------------------------------------
# IMAP with an app password, not the Gmail API — PRD §3.2 component 2. The API
# needs a Google Cloud project and an OAuth consent screen, and a self-hosted
# app left in "testing" gets refresh tokens that expire every seven days, so
# Steward would break weekly. An app password has no such trap.
#
# Google Account -> Security -> 2-Step Verification -> App passwords. Google
# shows a 16-character password once. **Paste it with the spaces removed** —
# Google displays it in four groups of four and the spaces are not part of it.
#
# The collector only ever reads envelopes: sender, subject, date. It never
# fetches a message body, so no mail contents are stored in Postgres.
#
# It collects unread in Primary and Updates, dropping Promotions, Social and
# Forums — Gmail's own categorisation does the filtering, so there is no rule
# list here to maintain. See SEARCH in src/lib/adapters/gmail.ts.
GMAIL_USER=you@gmail.com
GMAIL_APP_PASSWORD=
